WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.110 align:start position:0%
 
we're going to begin this hour with  another big story two years in the  making the emotional toll of the  pandemic on health care workers after  these two years of incredible stress a  recent study found that one in five  nurses say they are burned out some say  they struggle to eat to sleep and to  stay healthy themselves in a recent  survey an astounding 68 percent of  nurses have said they considered leaving  their jobs we spoke with five health  care workers all around the country  about the stress they face while on the  front lines  [Music]  working in healthcare is definitely a  marathon and it seems worse now you  don't get enough sleep  you're not eating and you're you're  working shift after shift it's just  exhaustion i might sleep for like four  hours  i kept replaying today over and over  again did i turn this off did i do this  correctly thinking back to march of 2020  i did not think we would still be here  now  the er that i work in we have 50 beds  that we can see patients in and we are  seeing  our senses at any given time is a  hundred so double the what we actually  have capacity for i come home from work  and there's days where my scrub top is  covered in sweat as soon as i'm walking  out of work because we're hustling you  know the entire 12 hours  you're seeing more death and dying than  ever before and that's hard and we are  people we're humans and we need time to  grieve too  when you're busy and your hospital is  bursting at the seams you don't have  that time you just have to go on and  take care of the next patient  the burnout has been around the  inability to get ahead of it so in  healthcare we're so used to being able  to get ahead of different things and a  lot of times we haven't been able to do  that because 19.  the most difficult part of  this whole pandemic for me has been  losing colleagues and friends to  different jobs or different  opportunities or to the burnout itself  i have friends that have gotten  completely out of nursing you've got a  shortage of workers you've got lots of  people out because they're quarantining  and so you you're having to pick up  extra in overtime in order to take care  of patients  we need more nurses in the workforce  [Music]  i got very lucky that my fiance does the  same job i do we work side by side every  day every shift we work is together it  definitely helps to have somebody who  fully understands  what i'm going through  i had that heart-to-heart conversation  with my husband and i told him that this  is what i love this is what i want to do  because i was on the fence of if i was  going to continue with this all  healthcare workers right now should  reach out and  be willing to open up to their  colleagues  to their friends to their families  that when they're feeling that burnout  we're so used to taking care of other  people we tend not to take care of  ourselves as much  [Music]  senior medical correspondent dr taran  narula joins us now uh dr naruella it's  powerful interviews there uh with  frontline workers they're all  experiencing some degree of burnout what  are the effects of burnout well i think  it's important that we say a lot of  people think this is a fluffy term but  actually in 2019 the world health  organization created a diagnosis code  for this and they call it an  occupational phenomenon and the roots of  burnout trace back to the 1970s when a  psychologist here in new york city was  volunteering at free clinics down in the  east village that were treating those  with drug addiction and he recognized in  himself and his staff what was happening  that they were really giving so much of  themselves they were becoming  emotionally depleted and they were  having a lot of the signs of what he  termed burnout he took that term from  the addiction community and then in the  1980s another psychologist expanded on  this christina maslak and actually  created what's now a burnout inventory  the mbi and that's how we grade it and  so we look at three main things it's  kind of characterized by emotional  exhaustion dissatisfaction with your  personal accomplishments and  depersonalization you're not connecting  with your patients the problem with all  this is that for healthcare workers it  really strips them it colors their world  the way that they see things the way  they interact with the world it takes  away hope and interest and joy and it  sets them up for things like substance  abuse depression suicide problems in  their relationships you know it's been a  couple of years you first did this story  a couple years ago where you focused on  the um the exhaustion of the medical  community i swear to you tara i never  even thought of that until you brought  it to our attention it it hurts that two  years later we are still having the same  conversation so what can hospitals do  what can they do what should they do to  address what is happening to health care  professionals and how tired and  exhausted and burned out they feel  because we need them absolutely and it  is a resource in our country that is  depleted right now and it was a crisis  that existed before covid we know that  one-third to one-half of health care  workers reported a burnout before covin  clearly that has increased now and i  remember we interviewed someone a year  or two ago a healthcare worker who said  he feels like a rope frayed at both ends  there was a recent article about burnout  that called it death by a thousand cuts  and that's really what it is you know  burnout when you ask healthcare workers  a lot of it is the administrative burden  the bureaucracy of charting and  paperwork but then add to that what's  happened this past two years right so  they're overwhelmed long hours staff  calling out staff sick and they're  filling in they had fear of being sick  themselves or bringing it home to their  family so what should hospitals do well  hospitals need to play a role in this  the leadership needs to create positions  like chief wellness officers they need  to create peer support groups they need  to create a culture where mental mental  health is okay to speak up about that  people won't feel penalized for talking  about it and they really need to create  resources flexible schedules even hazard  pay and bonuses so there's a lot that  leadership can do as well as individuals  yeah another reason why we should check  on each other especially the heroes that  work in healthcare that's right thank  you so much terry you're the best

